 "critic": "Cards that read as generated fell into a few repeating patterns. (1) Name-swapped templates: the Claws-fee message ('was at the stall again for the Friday fee the Claws collect, and I am short. Can you do something about it?') repeats verbatim with only names changed and gives no concrete ask or detail. (2) Terse bounty cards ('X wants Y out of the neighbourhood. Convince them. 1000 eddies.', 'Follow X and report who it is. 500 eddies.', sealed-case deliveries, 'has not heard from X in three days') are flat, with fixed prices and stock role tags such as 'a labourer'. (3) Long cards share a fixed skeleton: a complaint, then 'Do A, or B, or C', then a generic closing line about reputation or trust ('People trust you with their things...', 'You will not know me from anyone...', 'Everyone says you keep your head...'). The closers are interchangeable across scenarios and sometimes sit awkwardly with the body. (4) Repeated phrases such as 'somebody must have given the nod', 'no ties here' and 'I had never seen them before the fight' appear across many cards. The cards that felt more authored carried a concrete local detail or pressure: the stall collector at the till and the stalls starting to say no (H-073), patrols watching curfew rather than pickpockets (H-087), the crews watching each other (H-056), and the broken stall terminal (H-036)."
